ght not be able to beat landlord’s card and landlord wins. Riskier.  \n- **Play a triple**: If I play ♠7 ♥7 ♣7, landlord can’t beat triple with 1 card, teammate can’t either, so I get another turn, but I’ll still have cards left. Then I’d have to lead again.  \n\nGiven teammate is down to 1 card, if I play a **pair**, it’s safe: landlord can’t beat it, teammate likely can’t either (unless their last card is a rocket or bomb — impossible), so I retain control and can lead again next turn with a single that teammate might beat, hoping teammate’s last card is higher than landlord’s.  \n\nActually, **triple with single** is also safe, but uses more cards and may leave me with fewer options later. But since the game is near end, using more cards to reduce my hand helps.  \n\nBut if I play triple ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 now, and then on my next turn I lead with a single ♠4, landlord may beat it if they have a higher single, and teammate may or may not beat landlord.  \n\nBest choice: **Play ♠7 ♥7 ♣7** because:  \n1. Uses 3 cards, leaves me with 4 cards (two pairs: ♠4 ♣4, ♠10 ♥10).  \n2. Forces landlord and teammate to pass (since they have only 1 card each).  \n3. I get next lead, then I can play a **low single** to test landlord and possibly let teammate win if their last card is high.  \n\nChecking valid moves: triple: ♠7 ♥7 ♣7 is valid.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nThis move is safe, reduces my hand size, keeps initiative, and sets up a single lead next turn to put pressure on landlord while giving teammate a chance to win if their last card is higher.  \n\n**Answer:**  \n{"cards": ["♠7", "♥7", "♣7"]}'}
